 - `InvoiceCard.jsx:68`: reads `useAccount`/`useSignMessage` directly instead of going through `useAuth`, so it silently no-ops for UCW users.
 - `EscrowDetail.jsx:1872`: gated on `window.ethereum`, so UCW users get only a copy-calldata fallback for CCTP self-relay recovery.
 - `EscrowDetail.jsx`'s `ReceivingAddressEditRow` / `SplitAddressEditRow`: the "Receiving chain" dropdown offers every supported EVM domain plus Arc, even when the escrow (or split leg) currently pays on Arc — a direction F3 guarantees will revert, because those milestones were never floor-validated against the CCTP forwarding fee at deposit (`MilestoneBelowForwardFee` at `TrancheProtocol.sol:982` for the escrow-level path, which fires only when no splits are configured, and `:1033` per split leg). Cross-chain → cross-chain and anything → Arc stay allowed. The confirm screen now warns before signing — `redirectPayoutConfirm` / `redirectSplitConfirm`'s blocked branch — but the honest fix is filtering the unreachable domains out of `domainOptions`, built identically at `:859` and `:905`. Not done this round because it is a behaviour change, not copy. The same root cause surfaces a second time in the row's own `help` text — "You can update this anytime before the escrow is completed or cancelled" — which is equally unqualified. Both spots invite the user to commit to a redirect F3 will reject, and neither discloses the restriction until the final confirm screen.
-- `EscrowDetail.jsx`'s `SettlementPanel.propose()`: a failed live fee quote aborts the whole settlement. The function calls `resolveMaxFee` first and returns early on any throw, so a transient Circle fee-API outage blocks `mutualSettle` entirely — but `mutualSettle` never reads `maxFee`. The parameter is in the signature and absent from the body (`TrancheProtocol.sol:521-559`); the executing branch burns at the `e.escrowCctpForwardFee` snapshot instead, deliberately, so neither party can pick a fee that strands the other (settled decision #7). So the quote is fetched, passed, and discarded, and its only real effect is a failure mode on a transaction that does not depend on it. The fix is to stop gating this path on the quote — `resolveDispute` and the release paths genuinely need it, `mutualSettle` does not. Related: the same function computes its (discarded) burn estimate from the live `config.protocolFeeBps` rather than the escrow's snapshotted `escrowFeeBps`, which is harmless only because the result is thrown away.
